Bishop,

I've also experienced in the past here the niggling about Usenet
"rules" or "decorum", barely-disguised rudeness, grandiose
pronouncements of plonk list entry (who GIVES a shit whose messages
you decide to personally blacklist? - and normally the person you're
blacklisting cares least of all), the endless rationalizations for the
above behavior, etc., from several of the regulars and less-regulars.
I know the storyline too:  the newsgroup will descend into primeval
chaos if we don't whip posters into perfect syntactic form; and didn't
you read the FAQ???? - that's all the rationalization we need for
acting like asses.  This battle is replayed here with some regularity,
across different threads.  It almost always starts the same way, too.
An enthusiastic nethacker innocently posts a comment or question but
commits the sin of using one of the many varieties of Nonstandard
Formatting; then one of the newsgroup police pounce (usually ignoring
the content of the original post); then the OP either:  meekly backs
down, yes sir, I'm sorry sir, or takes exception to the tone and/or
content of the policeman's post and a situation like the present
ensues.  I've been there myself several times.

The best explanation I have is that the group is frequented by
programmers.  Not that programmers are a bad lot, but some extremely
anal programmers are here who tend to blur the boundary between their
black-and-white precise world of IDEs and variable declarations, and
the much more challenging grey world of human communication.  They
seem to believe that humans can, and should, be compelled to
communicate in well-defined protocols from which deviation is not
allowed.  They tend to misunderstand the difference between broad
guidelines for communicating and deviations from the norm which will
raise a syntax error.  They tend not to understand that
over-regulation creates antipathy and sometimes stifles willingness to
speak and creativity.  Sometimes, they seem afraid of Difference.

It's too bad.  This is an interesting and entertaining group with a
lot of great input about a game we love, but some of the worst cops
are some of the most frequent and knowledgeable posters.  I've lost
interest in communicating with them (and I probably couldn't if I
wanted to because of their +4 rustproof plonk-list armor).  I end up
still getting a lot of value from the group though, and I communicate
with the less uptight posters.

(Note to the cops:  This message was top-posted.  But if you read this
far, there was no degradation of signal because of the top-posting -
you got the message.  If you wish to reply, and quote contextually in
the format you prefer, it will take no more than a few seconds, some
barely perceptible finger-presses, and a couple quick whooshes of your
mouse to arrange things as you see fit.  And you won't have had to
pistol-whip anybody to accomplish it).

